==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|bazaz|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|bʰosós|lang=ine-pro}}.

''Germanic cognates:'' Old Frisian {{term|bar|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|bar|lang=osx}}, Middle Dutch {{term|bar|lang=dum}}, {{term|baer|lang=dum}} (Dutch {{term|bar|lang=nl}}, {{term|baar|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|bar|lang=goh}} (German {{term|bar|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|berr|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|bar|lang=sv}}, Norwegian Nynorsk {{term|berr|lang=nn}}).

''Indo-European cognates:''  Balto-Slavic {{recons|basas|lang=bat-pro}} (Lithuanian {{term|basas|bãsas|lang=lt}}, Latvian {{term|bass|lang=lv}}, Russian {{term|босой|tr=bosój|lang=ru}}, Polish {{term|bosy|lang=pl}}), Albanian {{term|mbath||I wear|lang=sq}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bær/|lang=ang}}

===Adjective===
{{ang-adj}}

# [[bare]], [[naked]], [[unconcealed]]
#: ''Wit her baru standaþ unwered wædo'': we stand here naked, unprotected by garments.  (Cædmon's ''Metrical Paraphrase'')

